{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwdZBCaT2h3L7tEmyiUe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't confirmed that the rule evaluates without errors; right after creation its status was \"pending\".\n\n**Data:** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` has `timestamp` as its time field. The main fields are `response` (a string, so I used `response.keyword`), `extension`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `host`, `url`, `request`, `referer` and `tags`.\n\n**Dashboard:** `EFF-2bc52323 Web logs overview` has four panels and a default time range of the last 7 days. Link: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-2bc52323-web-logs-overview-cd5b082fb9d4\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Requests over time (line)\n- Requests by response code (pie)\n- Average bytes by extension (bar)\n\n**Rule:** `EFF-2bc52323 5xx spike` is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute. It fires when more than 10 documents match a regexp on `response.keyword` (`5[0-9][0-9]`) within a 5-minute window. I didn't attach any actions or connectors, so when it fires it only shows up as an alert in Kibana and won't notify anyone. The rule ID is `193a2836-60c1-4383-87a2-38a7ae579032`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1244,"cache_read_input_tokens":19742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1244},"output_tokens":494,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4eb1cb7-866c-5bbf-8d81-a8d5acd83be3","uuid":"f77f5889-1f16-4275-9723-5676f24dcf1f","timestamp":"2026-10-12T03:26:54.717Z","request_id":"req_011CfwdZAygfPGBGbwMGCxag"}
